In Arch we've permanently moved to glibc>=2.42 so for
TCGETS2 the callers termios will always be equal or larger
than our ktermios. Code that runs on old and new glibc
would need to be more complicated than this.

Hopefully glibc tcgetattr.c ___termios2_canonicalize_speeds
knows how to correct mangled numeric speeds when we return
less.

The old glibc looks like it used TCGETS2 but TCGETS2 ==
TCGETS so our ptym code responded. The new glibc TCGETS2 is
a different constant so our code doesn't respond as if
tcgetattr() is not supported. glibc does not gracefully
fall back to TCGETS when TCGETS2 fails so we must respond
to it. We could have also wrote our own custom tcgetattr
that calls TCGETS but that seemed harder and less portable
than doing it this way.

New cfgetospeed code has not been tested on glibc<2.42 but
should work.

Watch the trueport baud rates in your favorite IOLAN
product WebManager, Statistics, Serial Ports, Serial Port
as you change baud rates in minicom.

-CJS severach@aur

Google AI helped in the making of this patch, not much, but
just enough to guide my view towards the problem and away
from non problems.

"How can I adapt a program written for pre glibc 2.42
termios to post glibc 2.42"

The necessary adaptations depend on how a program handles
serial speeds and legacy APIs:

1. Stop manually manipulating c_cflag

If your program sets baud rates by applying a bitmask or
directly modifying the c_cflag field inside struct termios
(a pattern common in very old code), it will break.

• The Fix: You must use the POSIX standard functions
cfsetispeed() and cfsetospeed(). Glibc internally bridges
these functions to handle the old kernel values or modern
custom BOTHER ioctls cleanly.

2. Guarding against Macro Value Assumptions
3. Replace <termio.h> references

4. Direct Custom Baud Handling (Alternative)

For cross-platform or hyper-portable tools that must
compile across varying versions of glibc and other Unices,
relying entirely on the native Linux kernel ioctl layer
(using TCGETS2 / TCSETS2 and struct termios2) is often the
most robust way to ignore glibc version discrepancies
entirely.

https://github.com/rust-lang/libc/issues/4692

